1
00:00:00,960 --> 00:00:05,520
This lecture will be split between memory consistency and SIMD architectures.

2
00:00:09,360 --> 00:00:13,120
We will first continue exploring shared memory multi-processors

3
00:00:13,120 --> 00:00:19,120
and discuss the concept of sequential consistency, then we'll get back to Flynn's taxonomy and cover

4
00:00:19,120 --> 00:00:27,360
SIMD and MISD parallel systems. Let's start by distinguishing between two terms,

5
00:00:27,360 --> 00:00:34,000
cache coherence and memory consistency. Coherence pertains to the ordering of multiple cores or

6
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:40,560
multiple threads, reads and writes to a single memory location. As discussed last time, enabling

7
00:00:40,560 --> 00:00:46,000
coherence implies that all accesses to a single memory location are ordered as if they were performed

8
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:53,200
in some sequential order. On the other hand, memory consistency pertains to the ordering of

9
00:00:53,200 --> 00:00:59,680
multi-threaded reads and writes to all physical memory locations. The most intuitive consistency

10
00:00:59,680 --> 00:01:05,360
model is sequential consistency, which like coherence enforces that accesses to all memory

11
00:01:05,360 --> 00:01:12,160
locations are ordered as if they were performed sequentially. This is the easiest model for

12
00:01:12,160 --> 00:01:17,040
programmers to understand, but like in-order cores, this can greatly constrain performance when

13
00:01:17,040 --> 00:01:21,920
threads want to read and write different memory locations that have no relation or dependence

14
00:01:21,920 --> 00:01:28,640
to one another. Instead, many modern systems employ relaxed consistency models that allow

15
00:01:28,640 --> 00:01:34,560
some reordering to happen so that we can improve performance. We'll only be discussing sequential

16
00:01:34,560 --> 00:01:39,840
consistency in this course. Relaxed consistency is left for advanced courses.

17
00:01:43,600 --> 00:01:49,760
Sequential consistency is met with two properties that we want to uphold. First, we want to enforce

18
00:01:49,760 --> 00:01:55,680
intra-thread program ordering of memory accesses so that each individual cores reads and writes

19
00:01:55,680 --> 00:02:00,880
occur in the order specified by the programmer. This means that even though memory addresses

20
00:02:00,880 --> 00:02:05,840
x and y point to different memory locations, we need to make sure that a load from x happens

21
00:02:05,840 --> 00:02:11,680
before a store to y, since that's what the programmer specified. Second, we want to enforce

22
00:02:11,680 --> 00:02:17,520
serialization of all reads and writes across all threads. As shown, this can be viewed as

23
00:02:17,520 --> 00:02:23,040
effectively having a single arbiter that allows only one core to access memory at any moment.

24
00:02:23,680 --> 00:02:30,000
If our multiprocessor system upholds these two properties, then we can say that our system is

25
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:39,760
sequentially consistent. Now let's go through some sequential consistency examples and show how

26
00:02:39,760 --> 00:02:44,640
even simple optimizations in our compiler and architecture can actually violate SC.

27
00:02:45,440 --> 00:02:50,560
Let's consider this example multi-threaded program running on two cores. If we assume that

28
00:02:50,560 --> 00:02:56,800
the two shared memory locations x and y are both initialized to zero, if our system were

29
00:02:56,800 --> 00:03:02,880
sequentially consistent, would it ever be possible for both a and c to be equal to zero at the end

30
00:03:02,880 --> 00:03:11,440
of the program? No, if you walk through all the cases, you'll find that it is not possible for

31
00:03:11,440 --> 00:03:18,000
a and c to be equal zero. If a ends up being zero, then p1 must have executed both instructions

32
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:26,800
before p2 stores five to y. This is because a can't get zero without x getting eight. Continuing on,

33
00:03:26,800 --> 00:03:33,840
that means that p2 must read the value of eight for x when it computes c, so it's not possible for c

34
00:03:33,920 --> 00:03:40,320
to also equal zero. On your own, walk through what a would be when c was zero.

35
00:03:44,560 --> 00:03:50,400
However, there's a very common compiler optimization typically made called common

36
00:03:50,400 --> 00:03:56,560
sub-expression elimination. The compiler is smart enough to recognize operations that are identical,

37
00:03:56,560 --> 00:04:02,960
such as this x times two computation. So if the compiler performs common sub-expression

38
00:04:02,960 --> 00:04:10,480
elimination, the compiler will simply replace the computation of c with b so that it doesn't do any

39
00:04:10,480 --> 00:04:17,520
redundant ALU work. Unfortunately, though, this seemingly harmless optimization actually violates

40
00:04:17,520 --> 00:04:24,000
sequential consistency. This eliminates the second load of extra memory, and now it is possible for

41
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:33,680
both a and c to end up being zero. Let's look at another example. If you were to go through this

42
00:04:33,680 --> 00:04:40,080
program, you'd find that it is impossible for all of a, b, and c to be equal to two at the end of the

43
00:04:40,080 --> 00:04:46,480
program. In fact, it isn't even possible for either a or b to be equal to two in the end.

44
00:04:47,200 --> 00:04:51,120
Please pause, walk through this, and confirm your understanding.

45
00:04:54,000 --> 00:05:01,040
But as you might expect, simple compiler optimizations may end up compromising sequential

46
00:05:01,040 --> 00:05:07,760
consistency. As before, common sub-expression elimination may be employed, replacing the

47
00:05:07,760 --> 00:05:15,360
second load of x with just a copy of the value and variable a. And then, since b is now directly

48
00:05:15,360 --> 00:05:20,480
being set to a, it seems obvious to the compiler that this if statement is always going to be true.

49
00:05:21,200 --> 00:05:26,880
So if it's always true, then we can just remove the branch entirely, saving some cycles and

50
00:05:26,880 --> 00:05:36,240
potentially avoiding mis-speculation. But now that we're left with just these three instructions,

51
00:05:36,240 --> 00:05:41,120
in the compiler's mind, the store to y has no data dependence with the previous two instructions.

52
00:05:42,560 --> 00:05:48,320
So our compiler may feel free to move the store to y earlier. And now, if we were to go through

53
00:05:48,320 --> 00:05:52,960
this optimized program, which seemingly performs the same computation as before,

54
00:05:53,600 --> 00:06:00,160
we'd find that sc can be violated, and indeed, all a, b, and c can equal two at the end of the program.

55
00:06:06,320 --> 00:06:10,800
Let's look at how our hardware optimizations can also affect consistency.

56
00:06:12,800 --> 00:06:16,480
In this program, it's clear that the assertion in p2 should never be false,

57
00:06:17,040 --> 00:06:23,680
because p2 would not enter the if block unless y were not zero. And looking at p1's code,

58
00:06:23,680 --> 00:06:27,760
y can only be set to one after having set x to one as well.

59
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:34,400
However, without of order execution and branch prediction, it's possible that the load of x

60
00:06:34,400 --> 00:06:40,320
and p2 can finish executing before the load of y in the branch condition. For example,

61
00:06:40,320 --> 00:06:46,960
p2's load of y may be a cache miss. But due to branch prediction, we may proceed and execute

62
00:06:46,960 --> 00:06:52,000
the load of x, which may be a cache hit. Then by the time we finally get the data for y,

63
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:57,440
it's possible that y may have been set to one. Thus, the branch prediction is deemed correct,

64
00:06:57,440 --> 00:06:59,760
even though we have read an older value of x.

65
00:07:02,640 --> 00:07:05,840
In this case, the assertion fails and sc is violated.

66
00:07:05,840 --> 00:07:13,760
Finally, we'll look at a more complex example with three threads running on a multiprocessor.

67
00:07:14,480 --> 00:07:18,640
p0 effectively sets the flag y that allows p1 to continue,

68
00:07:18,640 --> 00:07:22,400
and p1 sets the flag z that allows p5 to continue.

69
00:07:23,520 --> 00:07:27,200
In the end, it is not possible for b to read the value zero.

70
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:35,680
But let's consider what happens when running these threads on a real multiprocessor,

71
00:07:35,680 --> 00:07:40,000
where the cores are interconnected by some network. This means that for p0,

72
00:07:40,800 --> 00:07:47,840
p1 is closer than p5. Thus, coherence messages get delivered to it in less wall clock time than

73
00:07:47,840 --> 00:07:52,640
they do p5. So let's see how that affects the program.

74
00:07:55,920 --> 00:08:02,000
Say the first thing that happens is p0 stores to x, and this value properly gets propagated to p1

75
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:11,920
and p5's caches, as shown. Now assume that network congestion has built up in the system

76
00:08:11,920 --> 00:08:19,280
between p3 and p4 due to some unrelated programs. So now when p0 stores to y,

77
00:08:19,280 --> 00:08:24,160
p1 is made aware of this change immediately, while p5 still has no idea that the change

78
00:08:24,160 --> 00:08:30,400
has happened due to the congested network link. Then, since p1 is already aware of the modification

79
00:08:30,400 --> 00:08:35,920
of y, it now breaks from the while loop and continues executing its instructions and performs

80
00:08:35,920 --> 00:08:42,800
a store to z. Because its path to p5 is not congested, p5 notices the change to z before

81
00:08:42,800 --> 00:08:49,280
it notices the change to y. And because of that, we now end up with an impossible scenario of b

82
00:08:49,280 --> 00:09:00,320
being set to zero, violating sc. So how do we provide sequential consistency while allowing

83
00:09:00,320 --> 00:09:06,480
ourselves to benefit from these optimizations? The key is that to achieve sequential consistency,

84
00:09:06,480 --> 00:09:13,360
it only needs to look like these properties hold. To maintain intra-thread program order,

85
00:09:13,360 --> 00:09:17,760
we can tell the compiler specifically which loads and stores should not be optimized and

86
00:09:17,760 --> 00:09:23,360
reordered, while the rest can still be optimized freely. This applies to the first two examples

87
00:09:23,360 --> 00:09:29,600
earlier and can be done using the volatile declaration in the program code. To account

88
00:09:29,600 --> 00:09:34,000
for hardware optimizations like the out-of-order execution and branch prediction in the third

89
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:39,680
example, we can apply a mechanism of load replay, where we allow loads to be performed

90
00:09:39,680 --> 00:09:46,960
out of program order, as long as we double check them in order upon retirement. And finally,

91
00:09:46,960 --> 00:09:52,160
if we don't want to have to change our out-of-order hardware implementation to support replay,

92
00:09:52,160 --> 00:09:57,440
the programmer can manually insert fences to stall younger memory operations and force program

93
00:09:57,440 --> 00:10:05,120
ordering. Now, for inter-thread serialization, we recognize that we don't actually have to

94
00:10:05,120 --> 00:10:10,880
serialize reads across threads because they don't modify any values, so they can happen in parallel.

95
00:10:11,520 --> 00:10:16,480
So if that's the case, then inter-thread serialization can be simplified to just

96
00:10:17,760 --> 00:10:24,000
inter-thread write atomicity, that is, all writes are globally serialized. And to do this,

97
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:28,560
we need to make sure that all writes are made visible to all cores atomically,

98
00:10:28,560 --> 00:10:34,080
and we leverage the cache coherence protocol to do this. If one core modifies a block, we just need

99
00:10:34,080 --> 00:10:40,000
to make sure that our protocol waits for all other cores' acknowledgment of the modification

100
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:44,960
before we're allowed to proceed. We'll discuss each of these four mechanisms in a bit.

101
00:10:48,640 --> 00:10:52,880
The first mechanism is to use volatile declaration, which allows programmers to

102
00:10:52,880 --> 00:10:57,680
tell the compiler that a memory location may potentially be modified by some other thread or

103
00:10:57,680 --> 00:11:04,800
entity. So in this example, if memory address x is declared volatile, the compiler must assume

104
00:11:04,800 --> 00:11:10,800
that loads to x may not return the same value each time as it could be getting updated by a

105
00:11:10,800 --> 00:11:18,160
different thread. Thus, the compiler can't simply set b to equal to a, because during the time in

106
00:11:18,160 --> 00:11:23,280
between the two instructions, x may have been modified externally. This forces the compiler

107
00:11:23,280 --> 00:11:28,000
to be conservative and follow the programmer's explicit instructions and not optimize nor

108
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:36,720
reorder memory loads and stores for this address. So applying this to example two from earlier,

109
00:11:36,720 --> 00:11:41,760
we force the compiler to keep all the instructions the way they're written. With this, it is now

110
00:11:41,760 --> 00:11:47,920
impossible to have a, b, and c all equal to, which means no sc violation.

111
00:11:52,480 --> 00:11:57,920
The second mechanism was replay. We need to make sure that despite out-of-order execution,

112
00:11:57,920 --> 00:12:03,200
memory accesses in my thread should appear to execute in the order that they were specified

113
00:12:03,200 --> 00:12:08,640
by the program. This is no problem for stores, because as you recall from our discussion of MIPS

114
00:12:08,640 --> 00:12:16,240
R10K, stores are performed in order at retire instead of during execute. So we only need to deal

115
00:12:16,240 --> 00:12:21,440
with the loads, but we don't want to lose the benefit of executing loads out of order, since

116
00:12:21,440 --> 00:12:27,440
they can be very expensive, especially on a cache miss. So the idea is to go ahead and let loads

117
00:12:27,440 --> 00:12:32,240
execute out of order, as long as we replay the load again when it retires, just to make sure

118
00:12:32,240 --> 00:12:38,160
that the value has not changed since then. If it has changed, then we need to flush and re-execute

119
00:12:38,160 --> 00:12:45,680
all of the instructions that were dependent on the load. So in the example below, it is possible

120
00:12:45,680 --> 00:12:51,120
that the younger load may actually execute before the older load. This can violate sequential

121
00:12:51,120 --> 00:12:55,360
consistency if we weren't allowed to read the younger load's value before the older one.

122
00:13:00,160 --> 00:13:04,000
So to make sure there are no issues, we need to replay the younger load when it eventually

123
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:10,080
retires. This makes sure that the value hasn't changed since then. And this extra load operation

124
00:13:10,080 --> 00:13:15,280
is generally not expensive, since by the time we get to retire, the load value is most likely

125
00:13:15,280 --> 00:13:19,680
still in the cache, so it incurs minimal performance overhead to replay the load.

126
00:13:23,760 --> 00:13:28,880
So now we can apply this replay mechanism to example 3. With branch prediction and out of

127
00:13:28,880 --> 00:13:34,080
order execution, we will still let the load of x execute first, potentially reading the

128
00:13:34,080 --> 00:13:40,480
stale value as 0. But that's okay, because by the time this load retires, we will get the proper

129
00:13:40,480 --> 00:13:48,320
value of 1 since the load of y must have retired and returned 1 by now. Thus with replay, it is

130
00:13:48,320 --> 00:13:58,560
impossible for a to be equal to 0. One way to enforce intrathread ordering of loads and stores

131
00:13:58,560 --> 00:14:04,320
without changing the out of order hardware is to insert memory fences or memory barriers into

132
00:14:04,320 --> 00:14:11,360
the program. A general memory fence, denoted M fence here, ensures that all earlier loads and

133
00:14:11,360 --> 00:14:15,840
stores in this thread must be completed before any loads and stores are allowed to execute.

134
00:14:16,480 --> 00:14:22,560
This is a fairly severe constraint, and often it's sufficient to just have a fence for either loads

135
00:14:22,560 --> 00:14:28,960
or just stores. So a load fence stalls any future loads or stores to any address until

136
00:14:28,960 --> 00:14:35,440
all prior loads have retired. And a store fence stalls any future loads or stores to any address

137
00:14:35,440 --> 00:14:44,080
until all prior stores have been retired. So going back to example 3, we can ensure that

138
00:14:44,080 --> 00:14:51,680
sequential consistency is not violated by inserting appropriate fences. In P1, we insert a store fence

139
00:14:51,680 --> 00:14:56,320
to make sure that the store to address x completes before the store to address y is allowed to be

140
00:14:56,320 --> 00:15:02,640
executed. And in P2, the load fence makes sure that the load of y completes before the load

141
00:15:02,640 --> 00:15:13,600
of x is executed, thus preventing the SC violation. The last mechanism is coherence acknowledgments.

142
00:15:14,160 --> 00:15:19,120
We need to make sure that when a core modifies a value and transitions to the M state,

143
00:15:19,760 --> 00:15:24,560
all other cores first need to acknowledge this action before any core is allowed to read the

144
00:15:24,560 --> 00:15:30,640
new modified state. Note that for the snooping-based coherence implementations that we focus on in

145
00:15:30,640 --> 00:15:36,080
this course, it is implied that these writes are atomic since we're using a common atomic bus,

146
00:15:36,080 --> 00:15:40,080
and we assume that all cores are consistently watching and snooping the bus.

147
00:15:44,400 --> 00:15:48,880
However, if we didn't have a common atomic bus, here's what the coherence protocol would

148
00:15:48,960 --> 00:15:56,320
support to ensure right atomicity. When y is modified by P0, the congestion between P3 and

149
00:15:56,320 --> 00:16:03,200
P4 may slow down the notification to P5. But by ensuring that all nodes have to first acknowledge

150
00:16:03,200 --> 00:16:07,840
the write, then P1 will not be allowed to read the new value of y just yet.

151
00:16:10,400 --> 00:16:16,320
Only until P5 has acknowledged receipt of the notification will both P1 and P5 see the new

152
00:16:16,320 --> 00:16:25,520
value of y, which is 3. Only after that is P1 allowed to proceed and modify the value of z.

153
00:16:26,240 --> 00:16:32,640
But by the time P5 sees the modification of z, it is guaranteed to have seen the modification of y,

154
00:16:32,640 --> 00:16:35,840
ensuring sequential consistency is not violated.

155
00:16:40,320 --> 00:16:44,240
All right, that wrapped up our discussion on shared memory CMPs,

156
00:16:44,240 --> 00:16:49,840
which was our main focus for MIMD parallel systems. Let's return to Flynn's tectonomy

157
00:16:49,840 --> 00:16:53,280
to briefly cover the other paradigms, SIMD and MISD.

158
00:16:56,480 --> 00:17:01,920
In general purpose processors, SIMD parallelism takes the form of vector, or superword,

159
00:17:01,920 --> 00:17:07,280
instructions. The idea is to perform a single operation on multiple sets of data registers

160
00:17:07,280 --> 00:17:13,040
in parallel. In the example shown here, we have a vector add instruction that is able to add four

161
00:17:13,040 --> 00:17:20,000
registers with four other registers and write the result into four distinct registers, all as a single

162
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:28,400
instruction. To support vector instructions, the ISA needs to support vector registers.

163
00:17:29,040 --> 00:17:36,400
Generally, vector registers are composed of multiple regular registers. For example, shown here,

164
00:17:36,400 --> 00:17:42,880
vector register A can consist of four regular registers R0, R1, R2, and R3.

165
00:17:43,920 --> 00:17:48,720
These are registers can be used individually as operands for regular instructions.

166
00:17:48,720 --> 00:17:53,840
But to perform a vector operation, the program must first put all of its operands into one of

167
00:17:53,840 --> 00:18:01,440
the vector registers A, B, or C. In Intel's SSE vector instruction set, there are eight 128-bit

168
00:18:01,440 --> 00:18:08,800
vector registers that can be accessed as two 64-bit registers, four 32-bit registers, eight 16-bit

169
00:18:08,800 --> 00:18:19,120
registers, or 16 8-bit registers. To take advantage of vector instructions, the compiler

170
00:18:19,120 --> 00:18:25,520
usually employs two techniques, loop unrolling and loop fusion. Loop unrolling combines multiple

171
00:18:25,520 --> 00:18:30,720
iterations into a single larger loop body. If all of the instructions in the loop body are

172
00:18:30,720 --> 00:18:36,560
performing the same operation, there are four ads in this example, then the instruction can be

173
00:18:36,560 --> 00:18:43,440
converted into a single vector instruction. In this example, this can approach a speed up of four

174
00:18:43,440 --> 00:18:50,400
by vectorizing every four loop iterations. Loop fusion, on the other hand, merges multiple

175
00:18:50,400 --> 00:18:55,520
independent loops together into one larger loop. And if all the instructions in the loop body are

176
00:18:55,520 --> 00:19:01,200
performing the same operation, then they too can be replaced with a single vector instruction per

177
00:19:01,200 --> 00:19:12,560
iteration. Here we compare and contrast vector, or superword level, parallelism versus instruction

178
00:19:12,560 --> 00:19:19,840
level parallelism, which is the form of parallelism exploited by superscalar out-of-order processors.

179
00:19:20,800 --> 00:19:25,200
The downside of vector instructions is that the program first needs to move their data into

180
00:19:25,280 --> 00:19:30,880
dedicated vector registers. Then after executing the vector instruction, the results need to be

181
00:19:30,880 --> 00:19:35,840
moved to their intended destinations. This is known as register packing and unpacking,

182
00:19:35,840 --> 00:19:41,600
and can incur significant performance overhead. Further, vector instructions are less flexible

183
00:19:41,600 --> 00:19:46,960
since the same operation needs to be done for all operands. On the other hand, instruction level

184
00:19:46,960 --> 00:19:52,560
parallelism fetches and executes more total instructions. In the example shown, even though

185
00:19:53,280 --> 00:20:00,320
i1, i2, and i3 are independent and can be executed in parallel, we still need to fetch and execute

186
00:20:00,320 --> 00:20:06,640
three instructions and occupy three spaces in the ROB and other hardware structures. The advantage

187
00:20:06,640 --> 00:20:11,840
of vector instructions is that multiple operations are encapsulated as one single instruction.

188
00:20:12,480 --> 00:20:16,960
This is why the hardware complexity of vector instructions is much lower than a superscalar

189
00:20:16,960 --> 00:20:23,440
out-of-order core. So to make the hardware cost of a superscalar processor worth it,

190
00:20:23,440 --> 00:20:29,200
it needs to be utilized as much as possible by the program. If the program has too many dependent

191
00:20:29,200 --> 00:20:38,400
instructions, then the hardware cost may not be worth it. As an analogy, vector instructions are

192
00:20:38,400 --> 00:20:43,840
like a buy one, get one free deal. The discount is less flexible and is only really useful if you

193
00:20:43,840 --> 00:20:50,160
plan on buying multiple of the same product, whereas implementing a superscalar processor is

194
00:20:50,160 --> 00:20:56,160
like a membership discount. It's more flexible, but it imposes a high base cost and needs to be

195
00:20:56,160 --> 00:21:04,480
well utilized in order to be worth this cost. Building on the SIMD principle is the notion

196
00:21:04,480 --> 00:21:11,920
of single instruction multiple threads parallelism or SIMT. GPUs are the popular choice for SIMT

197
00:21:11,920 --> 00:21:16,560
execution. They were originally designed as shader cores for graphics programs where

198
00:21:16,560 --> 00:21:21,200
tons of threads are needed, and the threads basically all perform the same computation but

199
00:21:21,200 --> 00:21:27,040
on different data. And for the most part, each thread's computation did not have complex flow

200
00:21:27,040 --> 00:21:34,160
control and was just performing a bunch of arithmetic on its data. Today GPUs are effectively

